if β1 and β2 are in the vicinity of the critical fixed point (FP). Looking at the renormalized flow of σt, shown in figure 5 of reference [2] or in figure 1 of reference [4], one can notice—as already pointed out in [2]—that, starting above the critical temperature, σt decays and then, after a small number of steps t∗, reaches a ‘fixed point’ that is not the usual σ = 0 high-temperature FP, but has σFP > 0 and depends on the starting temperature. Analogously, starting below the critical temperature, σt grows and after t∗ steps reaches a ‘fixed point’ that is not the usual σ = ∞ low-temperature FP, but depends on the starting temperature. The reason for the existence of this plateau after t∗ is again the step (d) of the ERG procedure and it is thus completely unphysical, but it is just the consequence of an approximation introduced because of the finite-size of the analyzed systems. If we were dealing with systems of infinite size, starting below the critical temperature, σt would continue to grow indefinitely for t → ∞ and the plateau would not exist. For this reason in reference [2], equation (3) was used to extract ν, fitting data only for t < t∗, avoiding in this way to fit the ‘unphysical’ part of the RG flow affected by finite size effects. In the same paper it was also shown that t∗ grows if larger systems are used (for the ferromagnetic version of the model, the ERG method was applied up to n = 13), going to t∗ = ∞ in the n → ∞ limit. In this limit, step (d) is no more needed in the ERG procedure, and finite size effects disappear. The method for computing ν of reference [4] corresponds to the use of equation (3) in the limit t → ∞, as the author correctly pointed out. But taking this limit on systems of finite size, corresponds to study the evolution of the RG flow on the ‘unphysical’ plateau dominated by finite size effects, thus leading to an unphysical result for ν. In [4], the author correctly pointed out that fitting data with equation (3) for t < t∗, as we did in reference [2], takes contribution not only from λ1 but also from λi with i = 2, 3, 4 for n = 4. This is exactly the correct thing to do, because, in the large n limit, where step (d) of ERG is no more needed, the matrix M will not have the λ1 = 1 eigenvalue, but only the others eigenvalues λi with 2 i n. In reference [2] we showed how well an exponential fit of the form (3) fits the data obtained for the
